The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if ((s32) count < subsegments_per_sidx)
    subsegments_per_sidx = 0;

if (daisy_chain_sidx && (subsegments_per_sidx<2))
    subsegments_per_sidx = 2;
```
Reason:
The original logic incorrectly handled the `subsegments_per_sidx` variable when daisy chaining was involved. It did not properly account for the number of available fragments (`count`) in relation to the requested subsegments (`subsegments_per_sidx`). This could lead to situations where too few or too many subsegments were created, disrupting the intended functionality of movie fragment processing.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (daisy_chain_sidx) {
    if ((s32) count < subsegments_per_sidx)
        subsegments_per_sidx = 0;
    if (subsegments_per_sidx < 2)
        subsegments_per_sidx = 2;
} else {
    if ((s32) count <= subsegments_per_sidx)
        subsegments_per_sidx = 0;
}
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a conditional structure that correctly adjusts `subsegments_per_sidx` based on whether daisy chaining is enabled. It ensures that when daisy chaining is used, the logic accounts for the number of fragments available, preventing miscalculations in the number of subsegments created. This ensures that the intended functionality of the movie fragment processing is preserved.

